  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Question #2: Identify Positive and Negative Trends</title>
      <para>The scatter plot shows data on weight in pounds (x) and fuel efficiency in miles per
        gallon (y) for 13 cars.</para>

        <media
          alt="points (2500, 28), (3259,23), (3500,25), (3559,22), (3550,18), (3700,20), (4000,20), (4000,18), (4400,16), (4500,16), (4750, 14), (5250, 12), (5700,15)">
          <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_02_05_01.jpg" />
        </media>
      <para>Which statement best describes the data?</para>
    </section>

    <section>
      <title>Identify Positive and Negative Trends </title>
      <para>Scatter plots may have a strong linear trend or a weak linear trend. A strong positive
        trend means as one variable increases, the other variable increases, A strong negative trend
        means as one variable increases, the other variable decreases. A weak linear pattern means
        the points are more scattered.</para>
    
        <media alt="">
          <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_02_06_01.jpg" />
        </media>
 </section>
        <note class="try">
      <title>Try It: Identify Positive and Negative Trends</title>
        <media alt="">
          <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_02_06_02.png" />
        </media>
      
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Does the scatter plot show a positive or negative trend? Is the relationship weak or
            strong?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
            compare your work.</para>
          <para>Here is how to describe the relationship:</para>
          <para>As the \(x\)-values increase, the \(y\)-values decrease. The trend is negative. The
            relationship is linear, but the points are scattered, so the relationship is weak.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </note>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--inserted
      mini review question Begin -->
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="_33592150-3516-4f57-a9e6-6351e6ee7c9a" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>The scatter plot shows the relationship between crocodiles body mass in pounds,
              \(x\), and bite force in pounds, \(y\). Which statement best describes the
              relationship between body mass and bite force?</para>
              <media alt="">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_02_06_03.jpg" />
              </media>
                
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: There does appear to be a positive
              relationship, but that is because both quantities are increasing. The answer is there
              appears to be a positive relationship between bite force and body mass. The greater
              the body mass, the greater the bite force tends to be.</para>
              <para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: When there is a negative relationship, one quantity increases while another decreases. The answer is there appears to be a positive relationship between bite force and body mass. The greater the body mass, the greater the bite force tends to be.</para>
              <para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: looking at the scatter plot, as the body mass increases, so does the bite force. The answer is there appears to be a positive relationship between bite force and body mass. The greater the body mass, the greater the bite force tends to be.</para>
              <para>There appears to be a positive relationship between bite force and body mass. The greater the body mass, the greater the bite force tends to be.</para>
              <para>That’s correct! Check yourself: As the body mass of the crocodiles increases, so does the bite force. Since both quantities are increasing, there appears to be a positive relationship.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>

      <!--inserted
      mini review question END -->
